      "key": "team.reading.b1.city-parks",
      "kind": "ReadingPassage",
      "values": {
        "Title": "Protecting City Parks",
        "Content": "City parks give people fresh air and a quiet place to rest after work. Trees in parks also help reduce heat on hot days, which is useful in crowded neighbourhoods. Many families visit parks on weekends to walk, play, or simply sit under the shade. For children, open grass is often safer than busy streets.\n\nHowever, litter and damaged benches are common problems. When visitors leave bottles on the grass, the park quickly looks uncared for. Broken lights make evening walks feel unsafe. Local groups now organise clean-up days and plant new trees along the main paths. When citizens join these activities, parks stay safer and more beautiful for everyone who lives nearby.\n\nSchools can also teach students to respect shared green spaces. Teachers can include short park visits in science lessons about plants and animals. Small actions, such as using bins and staying on paths, protect parks for the next generation.\n\nIf more people take responsibility, city parks will remain valuable public places rather than neglected corners of the city. Local governments can support these efforts with clear rules and regular maintenance budgets that keep paths safe all year for walkers, runners, and families with young children who visit after school.",
        "Level": "B1",
        "Topic": "Environment",
        "ImageUrl": "asset:cf30cd002929e017b4a16c52ff7bf13d520bf83f5683d20d350f9e35692d3770.jpg",
        "IsPublished": true,
        "Description": "Why city parks matter and how people can protect them.",
        "EstimatedTimeMinutes": 12,
        "ExternalReadingKey": "RD-B1-003"
      },
